draft_notes.explanation:
The emitted program works directly in visible coordinates. Divider lines are rows or columns made entirely of palette[1]. If any whole row is the divider colour, the layout is vertical; otherwise it is horizontal. The program slices the grid into panels between the dividers.

The factory decides the panel order. It maps the native layout directions (+x for the horizontal layout, +y for the stacked layout) through the D4 transform: a horizontal reflection first, then clockwise rotations, with (dr,dc)->(dc,-dr). From the visible axis and the sign of the mapped direction, it knows whether the motif panel sits at the high end, so the panel list must be reversed. As a pixel-level consistency check, the motif panel must contain palette[0] (its border); the solid A/B panels never do.

The four roles are then:
- the motif: palette[4] cells, cropped to their bounding box;
- the bars: count the 4-connected components of palette[3];
- A: the dominant colour of the solid panel next to the bars;
- B: the dominant colour of the far solid panel.

The tile paints motif cells A and the other cells B. n tiles are laid along the visible layout axis with one-cell B separators. Every tile is identical, so the direction of tiling does not affect the result.
